      "reason": "`a` is fetched from the instruction stream (attacker-controlled operand byte, 0-255 directly or 0-65535 under EXT encoding) and used directly as a pool index with no check of the form `if (a < irep->plen)` or similar anywhere in this code; `len` is then taken from the same unchecked pool slot and passed to `mrb_exc_new(mrb, E_LOCALJUMP_ERROR, pool[a].u.str, len)` with no validation that `len` does not exceed the string's allocated size, so an instruction with an out-of-range pool offset reads out of bounds and the derived length can exceed the backing buffer, producing an out-of-bounds read/copy of attacker-chosen length inside the exception-message construction."
    }
  ],
  "notes": {
    "assumptions": [
      "The bytecode/irep being executed originates from a context where the instruction stream and pool contents are not previously validated, which is the standard trust model for a bytecode VM; if a separate loader elsewhere validates every OP_ERR operand against `irep->plen` before execution, this specific path would be guarded externally, though no such guarantee is quoted in the provided code.",
      "Line numbers refer to the layout of the provided code block with the function signature at line 1; if this is a fragment of a larger file, the absolute line numbers in that file will differ, though the relative position inside the OP_ERR CASE block is what identifies the defect."
    ],
    "limitations": [
      "This candidate was kept because the code performs no bounds check of its own: the STEP 5 search for every guard form (bounds check against `irep->plen`, length validation against a stored size, mask, clamp, corrected comparison, error arm) found none on any path to the `mrb_exc_new` call within this function.",
      "No other candidates (OP_JMPUW target computation, OP_ARGARY stack indexing, OP_BLKPUSH bounds logic) survived the counterfactual patch test, as each either has a quotable guard or its trigger precondition could not be instantiated from attacker data within this code alone."
