`code` is bounded by `bitmask` only up to (1<<n_bits)-1 ≤ (1<<BITS)-1, which is the table size — so `tab_prefixof`/`tab_suffixof` stay in bounds on the first iteration (mask + `maxbits > BITS` check). But this is the classic gzip unlzw.c bug: when `free_ent == maxmaxcode` (table full), the loop can walk a cycle (prefix chain that points to itself), decrementing `stackp` below `de_stack - ...` without bound — writing `*--stackp` out of bounds below `de_stack`. In the real gzip the defect is that the table-full condition (`free_ent < maxmaxcode`) is not rechecked/short-circuited when a code resolves to itself: `tab_prefixof(code) = oldcode` with `oldcode == code` makes `code = tab_prefixof(code)` loop forever.

Also note `oldcode` is assigned `incode` at loop end; entry write only when `(code = free_ent) < maxmaxcode`, so entries are in-range. The memory hazard:
- `stackp` is only guarded on the flush path by `if (outpos+(i = (de_stack-stackp)) >= OUTBUFSIZ)` — a check that assumes `de_stack-stackp >= 0`; if the walk descends below `de_stack`, `i` becomes negative and `memcpy(outbuf+outpos, stackp, i)` with negative length converts to huge size_t → massive OOB write.
- No check anywhere that `code != tab_prefixof(code)` (no cycle detection), and no bound `stackp >= de_stack - HSIZE` before `*--stackp`.

`tab_prefixof(code) = (unsigned short)oldcode;` — oldcode can hold a masked value ≥ table size only if input mask permits; mask bound keeps it ≤ maxmaxcode-1, in-range.

Line numbers: counting the code with line 1 = `int unlzw(in, out)`.

STEP 4 — trigger:
1. maxbits = 16 (attacker file byte), passes `if (maxbits > BITS)` (16 ≤ BITS).
2. n_bits grows to 16, bitmask = 0xFFFF, free_ent reaches maxmaxcode = 65535.
3. Table entries planted: `tab_prefixof(code) = (unsigned short)oldcode;` `tab_suffixof(code) = (char_type)finchar;`
